INSERT INTO `ha_term_relationships` (`object_id`,`term_taxonomy_id`,`term_order`) SELECT @translation_id,tt.term_taxonomy_id,0 FROM `ha_term_taxonomy` tt JOIN `ha_terms` t ON t.term_id=tt.term_id WHERE @translation_id IS NOT NULL AND tt.taxonomy='category' AND t.slug='iraq' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM `ha_term_relationships` r WHERE r.object_id=@translation_id AND r.term_taxonomy_id=tt.term_taxonomy_id) LIMIT 1;
INSERT INTO `ha_posts` (`post_author`,`post_date`,`post_date_gmt`,`post_content`,`post_title`,`post_excerpt`,`post_status`,`comment_status`,`ping_status`,`post_name`,`post_modified`,`post_modified_gmt`,`post_parent`,`guid`,`menu_order`,`post_type`,`post_mime_type`,`comment_count`) SELECT 1,NOW(),UTC_TIMESTAMP(),'<p>Broad bean is one of the most important pulses in biennial cultivation systems, attracting farmers\' attention due to its rapid growth and high nutritional value. However, one of the main challenges in growing this crop is the outbreak of pests and plant diseases, which can directly affect the quality and quantity of the harvest. In traditional irrigation methods such as flood irrigation, increased moisture levels at the soil surface and in the air around the plant provide an ideal environment for the growth of fungi and soil-dwelling midges. On the other hand, <strong>Pests and diseases of broad bean under drip irrigation</strong> management is significantly more effective, as these systems provide optimal moisture conditions for the root zone while keeping the aerial parts of the plant dry, thereby interrupting the transmission chain of pathogens. This approach is of great importance not only for plant health but also for water resource efficiency. In this article, the technical and practical details of managing the broad bean pathosphere using modern localized irrigation technologies will be examined to enable farmers to fully prevent the outbreak of damaging losses.</p><h2>Impact of Drip Irrigation on the Broad Bean Pathosphere</h2><p>The following specialized section examines in greater detail the physiological effects of drip irrigation on disease incidence. In broad beans, fungal diseases such as anthracnose, leaf spot, and root rot are among the most damaging factors. These diseases require high relative humidity on leaf surfaces and persistent wetness of fungal spore suspensions for their propagation. Narrow-tipped systems, by irrigating directly at the root zone and avoiding wetting the leaves, eliminate these conditions. Additionally, the reduced flow of moist air between plants in drip systems improves soil aeration, which reduces the deposition of fungal spores. This mechanism is vital for preventing secondary infection cycles during humid seasons. In fact, maintaining moisture balance at the soil surface without causing waterlogging inhibits the activity of pro-oxidant enzymes in plant cells and ensures the stability of delicate tissues. Therefore, the selection of appropriate droplet size and irrigation rate directly affects the level of environmental fungal pressure and, consequently, the damage inflicted on the final crop.</p><figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://navar-abyari.ir/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/navar-article-generated/آفات-و-بیماریهای-باقلا-با-آبیاری-قطرهای-تصویر-شاخص.webp" alt="آفات و بیماری‌های باقلا با آبیاری قطره‌ای - تصویر 1"/><figcaption>crop pests diseases drip irrigation broad bean</figcaption></figure><h3>Prevention of Root Diseases Through Trench Irrigation Management</h3><p>Broad bean root diseases are often exacerbated by suspensions of Fusarium or Phytophthora. Trench arrangements at appropriate intervals prevent excessive moisture accumulation at soil depth. Farmers should adjust the distance between trenches so that each trench evenly moistens its root zone without leaving any enclosed area with fluctuating moisture. This requires precise knowledge of broad bean rooting depth, which typically extends to 60 to 80 centimeters during the growing season. Precise soil moisture management at this depth is key to fighting soil-borne pests. If roots are continuously exposed to slow-draining moisture, biological oxidation is inhibited, and the roots become susceptible to anaerobic pathogens. To prevent this condition, the use of smart tilling for regulating moisture dynamics in the root zone is essential. These settings must be pre-calibrated based on soil type and field ambient temperature to prevent any waterlogging or sudden drought stress.</p><ul><li>Use of dual-trench layouts for better coverage of the root zone.</li><li>Adjusting system pressure to prevent root asphyxiation caused by water accumulation around the trench.</li><li>Periodic grading to ensure the soil surface of the trench remains dry.</li><li>Weekly monitoring of soil wetting depth using moisture sensors to adjust water application rates.</li></ul><h2>Insect Pest Management in Drip Irrigation Environments</h2><p>Although drip irrigation does not act directly as a chemical control method for insects, the ecological changes it induces affect insect behavior. Certain leaf-feeding insects, such as leafhoppers and thrips, proliferate rapidly under high humidity conditions. Keeping leaf surfaces dry with a drip system makes it harder for leaf-feeding insects to migrate. Furthermore, advanced drip systems can be integrated with fungicides and biological control agents, enabling direct delivery of these agents. In this context, a study on <a href="https://navar-abyari.ir/%D9%85%D9%82%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%A8%D8%B0%D8%B1-%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%82%D9%84%D8%A7-%D8%AF%D8%B1-%D9%87%DA%A9%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%B1/">Fava bean seed rate per hectare</a> can help farmers select an appropriate planting density to control insect outbreaks based on canopy coverage. Proper plant density increases spacing for air circulation. As canopy cover increases, airflow between plants is improved, and relative humidity at the lower canopy layers decreases. This reduces opportunities for spores and insect larvae to establish and multiply. Additionally, sunlight penetrates dense stands more freely, helping to eliminate certain vectors that require shaded, humid environments. Therefore, managing planting density alongside the irrigation system constitutes a dual-strategy for biological pest management, significantly reducing the need for chemical inputs.</p><figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://navar-abyari.ir/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/navar-article-generated/آفات-و-بیماریهای-باقلا-با-آبیاری-قطرهای-کاربرد-عملی.webp" alt="آفات و بیماری‌های باقلا با آبیاری قطره‌ای - تصویر 2"/><figcaption>crop pests diseases drip irrigation fava bean</figcaption></figure><h3>Practical tips for reducing pest damage</h3><p>To fully leverage drip irrigation for insect management, consider the following points:</p><ol><li><strong>Weed and cover crop management:</strong> In a drip system, the soil surface is kept free of weeds and ground cover, which are common hiding places for leaf-feeding insects. This feature reduces the need for direct chemical sprays on leaves.</li><li><strong>Top irrigation in the final month:</strong> In the late growth stages of fava beans, when plants are hardening off, gradually reducing irrigation frequency and increasing irrigation intervals prevents the spread of rust in the pods.</li><li><strong>Using movable tapes:</strong> Use of <a href="https://navar-abyari.ir/%D8%B4%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%B7-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D9%8A-%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AA%D9%86%D9%82%D9%8A%D8%B7-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A8%D8%B5%D8%B1%D9%87/">Irrigation tape for olives in dry areas</a> or durable polyethylene tapes helps prevent leaf-feeding insects from accessing the drip suspension through tape emitters.</li><li><strong>Synchronization with root-zone treatment:</strong> It is recommended to use fertigation systems to inject specific nutrients that enhance plant resistance to insect stress.</li></ol><h2>Comparison table: Drip irrigation vs. flood irrigation for disease control</h2><table border="1" style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse; border: 1px solid #ccc;">\n<tr><th style="padding: 8px; text-align: left; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Parameter</th><th style="padding: 8px; text-align: left; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Flood irrigation</th><th style="padding: 8px; text-align: left; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Drip irrigation / Tape drip</th></tr>\n<tr><td style="padding: 8px; text-align: left;">Leaf surface humidity</td><td style="padding: 8px; text-align: left;">Very high (suitable environment for fungal growth)</td><td style="padding: 8px; text-align: left;">Low (prevents fungal growth)</td></tr>\n<tr><td style="padding: 8px; text-align: left;">Soil aeration</td><td style="padding: 8px; text-align: left;">Poor (waterlogging)</td><td style="padding: 8px; text-align: left;">Good (precision moisture management)</td></tr>\n<tr><td style="padding: 8px; text-align: left;">Delivery of control agents</td><td style="padding: 8px; text-align: left;">Impossible or inefficient</td><td style="padding: 8px; text-align: left;">Feasible (soil injection/fertigation)</td></tr>\n<tr><td style="padding: 8px; text-align: left;">Damage from sap-sucking insects</td><td style="padding: 8px; text-align: left;">Higher (high humidity)</td><td style="padding: 8px; text-align: left;">Lower (relative dryness)</td></tr>\n</table><p>As shown in the table, the tape drip system offers an absolute advantage not only in water consumption but also in plant health. This advantage is particularly critical in humid regions where the prevalence of pea rust increases. To better understand the relationship between tape drip management and yield, studying the text \'Sharhe Sharit al-Riy al-Batqin fi Qasida\' can provide a deeper understanding of the principled design of irrigation strips under various soil and climate conditions. This information helps in selecting the most suitable model when purchasing equipment. Additionally, considering the confidence coefficient for weather variability during system design ensures that farmers can provide necessary root-zone moisture on hot and dry days without wetting the leaves and promoting leaf spot diseases.</p><figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://navar-abyari.ir/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/navar-article-generated/آفات-و-بیماریهای-باقلا-با-آبیاری-قطرهای-جزئیات-فنی.webp" alt="آفات و بیماری‌های باقلا با آبیاری قطره‌ای - تصویر 3"/><figcaption>crop pests diseases drip irrigation pea</figcaption></figure><h2>Conclusion and final recommendations</h2><p>Managing pea pests and diseases with drip irrigation is an integrated approach that requires precision in system design. Final recommendations include: First, adjust tape spacing based on the rooting depth of peas to prevent root drying or waterlogging. Second, use high-quality tapes with suitable emitters; for technical specifications of these tapes, refer to the page <a href="https://navar-abyari.ir/samak-jidar-sharit-al-ray/">Spray nozzle for drip irrigation tape</a> review the study. Third, if chemical control is required, use a combination of irrigation and root-zone spraying (Fertigation) to ensure active ingredients reach the root zone directly. Proper implementation of these measures will not only reduce fungal damage but also improve final crop yield. Use of <a href="https://navar-abyari.ir/%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%B9-%D9%86%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%AA%DB%8C%D9%BE/">Types of strip emitters</a> suitable to field conditions, is another step toward optimizing the entire chickpea field. Ultimately, continuous monitoring of plant status alongside the irrigation system is the only way to prevent sudden outbreaks of midges. These methods allow farmers to supply a healthy crop to the market with lower costs and higher quality. Investment in precision irrigation technologies will lead to reduced losses and increased profitability for chickpea farms in the long run.</p>','Pest and Disease Management in Broad Beans Under Drip Irrigation: A Comprehensive Guide','Drip and trickle irrigation play a key role in managing fungal diseases in broad beans. In this article, we explore practical strategies for reducing infections and improving the productivity of broad bean crops with a focus on moisture management.','publish','closed','closed','broad-bean-pest-disease-management-drip-irrigation',NOW(),UTC_TIMESTAMP(),0,'',0,'post','',0 WHERE @source_post_id IS NOT NULL AND @translation_id IS NULL AND @slug_conflict IS NULL;
